# Every `resource_type` written into `audit_log`, classified by whether it denotes a NATURAL PERSON.
#
# Why this file exists: GDPR erasure of the resource axis is the owning bounded context's job
# (docs/adr/audit-activity-log.md D4). That assignment only works if "is this type a person?" is a
# declared, reviewed decision — otherwise a new person-denoting type ships with nobody erasing it, and
# the subject's real id survives their own erasure beside their pseudonym.
#
# Format, one per line:
#   <ResourceType> => non-person
#   <ResourceType> => person :: <path of the erasure use case that anonymises it>
#
# The gate (PersonResourceErasureGateTest, run by `make php.lint.audit-resource`) fails the build when a
# type reaches `AuditResource::of(...)` — written at the call or held in a same-class constant — or a
# route's `_audit_resource_type` default without a line here, and when a `person` line names a file that
# does not call anonymise() on an AuditResourceAnonymiser it holds.
#
# What it does not catch, stated so nobody mistakes a green build for a proof: it cannot judge the
# classification (calling Contact a non-person passes — that is what review is for), it cannot see a type
# assembled at runtime from request attributes, and it cannot follow a constant imported from another
# class. A type introduced by either of those last two forms needs a line here by hand.

Bank => non-person
BankAccount => non-person
